{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"802af055af63139dc6fdbe92983739a55e89d52e542c614615261d2263d4f7c2","slug":"index","location":"/intro.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"From Models to Learning","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Pierre-Luc Bacon","given":"Pierre-Luc","family":"Bacon"},"name":"Pierre-Luc Bacon","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"github":"https://github.com/pierrelux/rlbook","math":{"\\Proj":{"macro":"\\mathsf{P}"},"\\Residual":{"macro":"\\mathsf{N}"},"\\Contraction":{"macro":"\\mathsf{T}"},"\\Bellman":{"macro":"\\mathsf{L}"},"\\BellmanPi":{"macro":"\\mathsf{L}_\\pi"},"\\BellmanQuad":{"macro":"\\widetilde{\\mathsf{L}}"},"\\EulerResidual":{"macro":"\\mathsf{H}"},"\\Policy":{"macro":"\\pi"}},"source_url":"https://github.com/pierrelux/rlbook/blob/main/intro.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/pierrelux/rlbook/edit/main/intro.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"intro.md","url":"/rlbook/build/intro-5ba423de664fdbb5162d1cbd3ce63692.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reinforcement learning provides a precise language for decisions whose\nconsequences unfold over time. Once a state, a set of actions, a reward, and a\ntransition interface have been specified, it asks how models or experience can\nbe used to evaluate and improve a policy. This abstraction is one of the field’s\nstrengths: the same mathematical ideas can describe games, robots, treatment\npolicies, and resource allocation.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"K27BufIBS5"}],"key":"eRuTTffJqD"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definition used in this course","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"espx2bpvRq"}],"key":"jTBAh6a7j9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reinforcement learning","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"Pwctmh1qgO"}],"key":"ArInPWum56"},{"type":"text","value":" is the study of systems that learn from data how to\nmake good decisions in a dynamical system: a system whose future evolution can\nbe affected by present actions.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"IREteLHTQP"}],"key":"A05kIcDp3I"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This names a problem class. Temporal-difference learning, policy-gradient\nmethods, and value-based methods are important algorithm families within it.\nWhat counts as a good decision is determined by the objective and constraints in\nthe formulation.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"vCuNOPiq3A"}],"key":"eLLHrVSAU8"}],"class":"course-definition","key":"Ymm6kBdQ8b"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"An application does not arrive with that interface attached. Someone must decide\nwhere the system ends, which information is available when an action is chosen,\nwhich interventions are physically possible, how success is measured, and which\nrequirements cannot be traded for reward. Each choice is a claim about the\nsystem. An optimizer can act only on the variables, objectives, and constraints\nsupplied by the formulation. An omitted requirement cannot affect the computed\nsolution.","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"jbE3jk580p"}],"key":"IQvSii4NuH"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Agent-Environment Interface","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"ZdjM6MtZr6"}],"identifier":"the-agent-environment-interface","label":"The Agent-Environment Interface","html_id":"the-agent-environment-interface","implicit":true,"key":"ngUReNKm7O"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"narrative","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"narrative","label":"SuttonBarto2018","identifier":"suttonbarto2018","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sutton & Barto (2018)","key":"mj8QCYf4L5"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"LQsyyjxsUz"}],"key":"KdKZ4UHChd"},{"type":"text","value":" are explicit about the scope of their abstraction. The\nagent-environment boundary is determined after particular states, actions, and\nrewards have been selected. They also take the state signal as the output of a\npreprocessing system and largely set aside the problem of constructing it so\nthat they can focus on decision making. This is a deliberate and productive\ndivision of labor, not a claim that representation or formulation is\nunimportant.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"I2iZtac5Ld"}],"key":"Qpkny0lFFD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The familiar agent-environment loop also appears in other fields. Control\ntheory, operations research, and econometrics use different names for related\ncausal exchanges and ask different questions of them.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"S2FajwXqjA"}],"key":"gMkDhuAz4y"},{"type":"div","identifier":"fig-interaction-loop-vocabularies","label":"fig-interaction-loop-vocabularies","class":"interaction-lenses-figure","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lenses","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lens","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lens-title","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reinforcement learning","key":"y4CWE3gHR3"}],"key":"SSVpW9Q2m7"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-loop","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-node","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent","key":"opRuW6snJ0"}],"key":"aqv8hefgoh"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"P07kP1bKm6"},{"type":"span","class":"interaction-node-subtitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"policy ","key":"V4bCGUchaO"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"π","key":"TWoBHcJh5A"}],"key":"fgd5237L4m"}],"key":"J3teMJlRZr"}],"key":"X5THt127Ux"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-arrows","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"action ","key":"naOYm2Rjxs"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A","key":"NhMvo1tGLR"}],"key":"s4gGCJOxZI"},{"type":"subscript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"t","key":"GJ7a9pU7yl"}],"key":"rcRiQ9vkIb"}],"key":"b1bSgd8Hwx"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-arrow-glyph","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"→","key":"KQujRAE82O"}],"key":"c0Vog7MsDG"},{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"←","key":"ZCtlsMnnCG"}],"key":"Hvh8nHNxks"}],"key":"tEedejRaN5"},{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"state ","key":"sDNjdUbsik"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"S","key":"vBbrvBncE5"}],"key":"bBY2tEz88s"},{"type":"subscript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"t+1","key":"J6GAjXxGbN"}],"key":"Nz8deCtG4W"},{"type":"break","key":"uG38eSz3At"},{"type":"text","value":"reward ","key":"bXIUH8McGn"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R","key":"k9meoURUp8"}],"key":"RnhFpiS3E9"},{"type":"subscript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"t+1","key":"LkDqVseDJq"}],"key":"cGuQZuvpRn"}],"key":"EfvZ9IAbQk"}],"key":"YezkbYySwZ"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-node interaction-node-world","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Environment","key":"FLS9cALRQm"}],"key":"mcDbiQhlwu"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"jAtLqk7X9Q"},{"type":"span","class":"interaction-node-subtitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"transition ","key":"f9CgXEAUVP"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"p","key":"ocfC2gOqzg"}],"key":"MEMg6h3gTy"}],"key":"uu2ZxnmEpc"}],"key":"WVOROQoEeJ"}],"key":"ohPtcU2ULK"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How can models or experience evaluate and improve a policy?","key":"u0lAjH3PXh"}],"key":"oLkHQfkpoT"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"objective encoded by reward","key":"hX5LHfSFR1"}],"key":"fO4zfWfckz"}],"key":"DkBmyD3rQ2"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lens","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lens-title","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Control theory","key":"ukbWRD5lyI"}],"key":"WjiK9ZwSnN"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-loop","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-node","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controller","key":"qYWkPKFran"}],"key":"fFBRl3IybM"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"Ja6MwBPKHF"},{"type":"span","class":"interaction-node-subtitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"feedback law ","key":"f5LjVUtFku"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"κ","key":"nEt8u9Enk3"}],"key":"W8IIjU1T8H"}],"key":"e4ti9MpxhA"}],"key":"NwQrJEYKmt"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-arrows","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"input ","key":"iUfT5RcLtd"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"u","key":"SxHIwlUlkR"}],"key":"kwDhoaRyM5"},{"type":"subscript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"t","key":"DeSodndnBb"}],"key":"WK0N0AoLnp"}],"key":"WJbxo2KzKi"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-arrow-glyph","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"→","key":"soctzY1eUH"}],"key":"qWtkG2Q0W4"},{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"←","key":"BZYTI1O8yB"}],"key":"bcreFxQO3i"}],"key":"VnlZDuley8"},{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"measurement ","key":"xr8MMLWSlI"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"y","key":"RPqWg0Gin6"}],"key":"tyLdoWt7Sp"},{"type":"subscript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"t","key":"hYzizK2p1i"}],"key":"By4WoyEhgz"}],"key":"BHwIirnCJk"}],"key":"Irc9eFRG9s"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-node interaction-node-world","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Plant","key":"jh5PoIaRJY"}],"key":"BBwCg2TPgg"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"YoDM4hz4Kl"},{"type":"span","class":"interaction-node-subtitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"dynamics ","key":"TvmEnQN8FL"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"f","key":"bjwNKUc4XK"}],"key":"N3XkQlaJnC"}],"key":"u1wMjigWbK"}],"key":"e0FeD7bFct"}],"key":"DGGZ5eNlTE"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which feedback law makes the plant meet its specification?","key":"OCERBKskBP"}],"key":"bRNnNs7exo"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reference · disturbances · cost · constraints","key":"iJcFvNWAyw"}],"key":"CHAOSzM0Za"}],"key":"oOVjwf0458"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lens","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lens-title","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OR and econometrics","key":"mKqPWmq9Dn"}],"key":"hvBqFlHVzf"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-loop","children":[{"type":"div","class":"interaction-node","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decision rule","key":"zFLZSENbLs"}],"key":"ByKGEcrvSY"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"TMlyxI7Bfz"},{"type":"span","class":"interaction-node-subtitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"objective + constraints","key":"P9iMCrPnqo"}],"key":"oYmgCd00vi"}],"key":"zJ8tcS2y2I"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-arrows","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"decision ","key":"q30EpbtRe0"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"d","key":"AOdUa56nHH"}],"key":"QtxqQsaW0V"},{"type":"subscript","children":[{"type":"text","value":"t","key":"NiVCHygDoj"}],"key":"fk1IF2AQWe"}],"key":"mAMtR2s9AN"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-arrow-glyph","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"→","key":"rUyyxUuMq6"}],"key":"BZGBfCrnal"},{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"←","key":"FihUtNEBrc"}],"key":"zf22bddg8k"}],"key":"bFAx1NFIw4"},{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"covariates and","key":"nfJI3oqT1t"},{"type":"break","key":"O39XVmarKv"},{"type":"text","value":"outcome","key":"dEBU4l3v03"}],"key":"TTQdRML7M9"}],"key":"FjJg9a7EN6"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-node interaction-node-world","children":[{"type":"span","children":[{"type":"text","value":"System or population","key":"RFb3ksDYA2"}],"key":"DHbjmt1GEU"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"L0Em1Y7UvB"},{"type":"span","class":"interaction-node-subtitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"data-generating process","key":"KpiQfoCmqu"}],"key":"cTOl1rwEPy"}],"key":"bhIuXWo7B1"}],"key":"QFo331uSWg"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What would each feasible intervention cause, and which should be selected?","key":"tGsVZBopnq"}],"key":"HVkenA408S"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"shocks · feasibility · causal assumptions · welfare","key":"FYqFDmpx1s"}],"key":"xhyl5et7RF"}],"key":"mEBXxfRjYV"}],"key":"lYELoiaROI"},{"type":"div","class":"interaction-lenses-caption","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three vocabularies for a shared causal interaction.","key":"outwOpuXFU"}],"key":"rlIdgUxY6z"},{"type":"text","value":" Reinforcement learning describes an agent acting in an environment; control theory describes a controller acting on a plant; operations research and econometrics describe decisions or interventions applied to an evolving system. The correspondence is structural rather than exact: a measurement need not reveal the state, a reward or cost must be specified, and econometric counterfactuals require identification assumptions. The reinforcement-learning panel follows the interaction convention of Sutton and Barto (2018).","key":"nsLvlSKxZI"}],"key":"IxaIMLcxZ8"}],"html_id":"fig-interaction-loop-vocabularies","key":"DpO0dsLQdy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":127,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This book studies both sides of that division. It develops the standard theory\nfor choosing actions after a sequential decision problem has been specified. It\nalso studies how that problem is obtained from a system whose dynamics may be\npartly known, whose observations may be incomplete, and whose actions and\noutcomes are constrained. Its distinctive emphasis is the reasoning that\nconstructs and audits the decision problem, selects a solution method, and\nevaluates the resulting policy.","position":{"start":{"line":121,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"key":"GUMOrqZGyH"}],"key":"hNh6LHUhGd"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":129,"column":1},"end":{"line":137,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The distinction matters outside benchmark environments. A study of real-world\nreinforcement learning identifies limited samples, delays, high-dimensional\nstate and action spaces, safety constraints, partial observability,\nstochasticity and nonstationarity, multi-objective or poorly specified rewards,\nreal-time action selection, offline logs, and explainability to system operators\nas recurring challenges ","position":{"start":{"line":129,"column":1},"end":{"line":129,"column":1}},"key":"ZCeAeHjYjr"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"parenthetical","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"parenthetical","label":"DulacArnold2021","identifier":"dulacarnold2021","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dulac-Arnold ","key":"cYtAfHfqrM"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"ute8Fk08Cg"}],"key":"YWv0pWBUv0"},{"type":"text","value":", 2021","key":"DhuJ2GfKAe"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"Fger7lH2P8"}],"key":"rlSPeZTSjA"},{"type":"text","value":". These are not independent\ndetails added after an algorithm has been selected. They determine the state,\naction, model, objective, data, and evaluation protocol on which the algorithm\noperates.","position":{"start":{"line":129,"column":1},"end":{"line":129,"column":1}},"key":"PPEiSHXTp8"}],"key":"VVN0ZU5wGl"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":139,"column":1},"end":{"line":139,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Magnetic Control on the TCV Tokamak","position":{"start":{"line":139,"column":1},"end":{"line":139,"column":1}},"key":"aMgYNOUrt4"}],"identifier":"magnetic-control-on-the-tcv-tokamak","label":"Magnetic Control on the TCV Tokamak","html_id":"magnetic-control-on-the-tcv-tokamak","implicit":true,"key":"QEkK4sy7Tf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":141,"column":1},"end":{"line":145,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"TCV is a fusion-research device that confines plasma using magnetic fields. In\nthe controller developed by ","position":{"start":{"line":141,"column":1},"end":{"line":141,"column":1}},"key":"Mpf5QPO0Dp"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"narrative","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"narrative","label":"Degrave2022","identifier":"degrave2022","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Degrave ","key":"Y8o6sw25wY"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"MYePzyB3Bs"}],"key":"PwayPGJPWJ"},{"type":"text","value":" (2022)","key":"tQcqapgbAp"}],"enumerator":"3","key":"AxRHxVpRRP"}],"key":"qABQ3cGGr6"},{"type":"text","value":", conventional control first\nformed and stabilized the plasma. A learned policy then issued voltage commands\nto its 19 control coils at 10 kHz and controlled several plasma configurations\non the physical machine.","position":{"start":{"line":141,"column":1},"end":{"line":141,"column":1}},"key":"jVvlpQoS0i"}],"key":"l3ZqaLaNlO"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":147,"column":1},"end":{"line":151,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simulator combined conductor-circuit dynamics, a free-boundary plasma model,\nand lumped plasma-current dynamics. Data-identified sensor and power-supply\nmodels represented filtering, delays, measurement noise, and voltage offsets.\nUncertain physical parameters were varied over experimentally informed ranges,\nand objectives and termination conditions encoded operating requirements.","position":{"start":{"line":147,"column":1},"end":{"line":147,"column":1}},"key":"U2Md2fi6eQ"}],"key":"GREed5mo6F"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":153,"column":1},"end":{"line":159,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The learned policy became the feedback controller after handover. The surrounding\nsystem fixed the simulator, observations, action interface, objectives,\ntermination rules, randomized physical parameters, and handover state. In this\nexperiment, reinforcement learning used physical models and conventional\nplasma-formation control rather than replacing them. The example does not imply\nthat every application needs a detailed physical simulator. It shows that using\nlearning does not remove the need to identify and exploit available structure.","position":{"start":{"line":153,"column":1},"end":{"line":153,"column":1}},"key":"vKgselEF82"}],"key":"oDsVNdMorp"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":161,"column":1},"end":{"line":165,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"narrative","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"narrative","label":"Brunke2022","identifier":"brunke2022","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Brunke ","key":"nRhKEb727w"},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"et al.","key":"CqFDTEp1Kz"}],"key":"ZrDA9JDTfH"},{"type":"text","value":" (2022)","key":"KwTsTwJNQL"}],"enumerator":"4","key":"O52IzSFWoQ"}],"key":"FYaX1LIIG9"},{"type":"text","value":" formalize the same division for safe robot control by\ndecomposing dynamics, cost, and constraints into nominal components that encode\nprior knowledge and uncertain components learned from data. Their review treats\nmodel-driven and data-driven approaches as endpoints and studies methods that\ncombine them.","position":{"start":{"line":161,"column":1},"end":{"line":161,"column":1}},"key":"meob9tbCQ7"}],"key":"LYYvtN0ZeW"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":167,"column":1},"end":{"line":167,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Relation to Standard Reinforcement Learning","position":{"start":{"line":167,"column":1},"end":{"line":167,"column":1}},"key":"pMvoVDzECy"}],"identifier":"relation-to-standard-reinforcement-learning","label":"Relation to Standard Reinforcement Learning","html_id":"relation-to-standard-reinforcement-learning","implicit":true,"key":"XIowmeK9ts"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":169,"column":1},"end":{"line":175,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A reinforcement-learning course centered on ","position":{"start":{"line":169,"column":1},"end":{"line":169,"column":1}},"key":"kmcyuPrLC9"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"narrative","children":[{"type":"cite","kind":"narrative","label":"SuttonBarto2018","identifier":"suttonbarto2018","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sutton & Barto (2018)","key":"Dd4yQskzey"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"pV5aSxQg41"}],"key":"VYjKW0SqDT"},{"type":"text","value":" develops\ngeneral solution methods once the sequential decision interface has been chosen:\nBellman equations, dynamic programming and planning, Monte Carlo and\ntemporal-difference methods, value-function approximation, and policy gradients.\nThese foundations are also central to this book. The distinction is therefore\nnot between model-free and model-based reinforcement learning; the standard\ntheory contains both.","position":{"start":{"line":169,"column":1},"end":{"line":169,"column":1}},"key":"pCmhws3qua"}],"key":"lCKyKdiwQl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":177,"column":1},"end":{"line":186,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The difference is one of starting point and emphasis. This course also studies\nthe modeling and control steps that construct and surround the standard\nreinforcement-learning interface. When dynamics and constraints are known,\ntrajectory optimization may produce a useful plan directly. Replanning can turn\nthat plan into feedback, and a local linear model may be sufficient for\nstabilization. Dynamic programming becomes useful when computation must produce\nstate-contingent decisions across many states or stochastic future branches.\nLearning becomes useful when a required model component, value function, or\npolicy must be inferred from data, or when an exact computation must be replaced\nby an approximation.","position":{"start":{"line":177,"column":1},"end":{"line":177,"column":1}},"key":"Yt7wGXL9Uw"}],"key":"D7s5AsWTZf"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":188,"column":1},"end":{"line":193,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When only transition samples and rewards are available, sample-based\nreinforcement-learning methods can operate directly on that interface. When\nequations, geometry, conservation laws, or hard constraints are also available,\nreplacing them all with samples discards directly usable information. Partial\nmodels lead to a third case: known structure remains explicit while data are used\nfor uncertain parameters, residual dynamics, value functions, or policies.","position":{"start":{"line":188,"column":1},"end":{"line":188,"column":1}},"key":"HQkzhQbuX6"}],"key":"K27m0fGAby"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":195,"column":1},"end":{"line":200,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The course therefore prepares students to move between formulations. They\nshould be able to inspect a new sequential decision problem, identify what is\nknown, choose a computation that uses that information, state what must be\nlearned, and design evidence that could reveal a modeling failure. This scope\ncomplements a standard RL curriculum. It does not replace the algorithms or\ntheory developed there.","position":{"start":{"line":195,"column":1},"end":{"line":195,"column":1}},"key":"VE0kBHrzuB"}],"key":"AH34VarOoe"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":202,"column":1},"end":{"line":202,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Formulating the Decision Problem","position":{"start":{"line":202,"column":1},"end":{"line":202,"column":1}},"key":"kRaURVxFHD"}],"identifier":"formulating-the-decision-problem","label":"Formulating the Decision Problem","html_id":"formulating-the-decision-problem","implicit":true,"key":"b1bzEEFqzU"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":204,"column":1},"end":{"line":205,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The problem is rarely handed to us in finished form. A useful formulation must\nanswer five questions:","position":{"start":{"line":204,"column":1},"end":{"line":204,"column":1}},"key":"c2ytju8hbq"}],"key":"SFE73u0FhT"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":207,"column":1},"end":{"line":212,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":207,"column":1},"end":{"line":207,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which variables summarize the information needed for future decisions?","position":{"start":{"line":207,"column":1},"end":{"line":207,"column":1}},"key":"tCGlEBvuWQ"}],"key":"FDZ2GkCZnb"}],"key":"GeGOJFYJXs"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":208,"column":1},"end":{"line":208,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which actions can the decision maker actually apply?","position":{"start":{"line":208,"column":1},"end":{"line":208,"column":1}},"key":"GZreJwmrvu"}],"key":"lhtEs6eWAc"}],"key":"Gn8Q0IR8GA"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":209,"column":1},"end":{"line":209,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do actions, disturbances, and uncertainty change the system?","position":{"start":{"line":209,"column":1},"end":{"line":209,"column":1}},"key":"X7s8I1AWgz"}],"key":"b1LFsS6gc6"}],"key":"VupLnxOsxF"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which outcomes should the objective reward or penalize?","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"key":"PFdYVqKUg9"}],"key":"AihftRlwqI"}],"key":"fmELvmx0Gk"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":211,"column":1},"end":{"line":212,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which physical, operational, or safety constraints must always hold?","position":{"start":{"line":211,"column":1},"end":{"line":211,"column":1}},"key":"FB2GgliAYB"}],"key":"SpmzMW8EeA"}],"key":"oLKgfbHtoV"}],"key":"ayIBRUneth"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":213,"column":1},"end":{"line":217,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The answers determine which computations are available. An explicit\ndifferential equation can expose derivatives and conservation laws. A simulator\nmay provide only trajectory samples. Logged data cannot answer arbitrary\ncounterfactual queries without additional assumptions. Interaction provides new\ndata, but it also consumes time and can damage the system.","position":{"start":{"line":213,"column":1},"end":{"line":213,"column":1}},"key":"q6rMAjouzn"}],"key":"PcRXCpuAce"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":219,"column":1},"end":{"line":219,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Course Progression","position":{"start":{"line":219,"column":1},"end":{"line":219,"column":1}},"key":"tQf25u0Ylp"}],"identifier":"course-progression","label":"Course Progression","html_id":"course-progression","implicit":true,"key":"SERpJVVpOh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":221,"column":1},"end":{"line":224,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The technical development follows the information available about the decision\nproblem. Every later method depends on some account of how actions lead to future\nobservations, whether supplied by equations, a simulator, logged transitions, or\nlive interaction.","position":{"start":{"line":221,"column":1},"end":{"line":221,"column":1}},"key":"v2W86y7vN6"}],"key":"pHb5v7NIuh"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":226,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":226,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Part","position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":226,"column":1}},"key":"W3M0rHe2S8"}],"key":"ADUBcSAzFC"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":226,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Question","position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":226,"column":1}},"key":"hJ7iq5JJxB"}],"key":"F4zHA5jvfH"},{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":226,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result","position":{"start":{"line":226,"column":1},"end":{"line":226,"column":1}},"key":"fKMrYVU2Zw"}],"key":"iL8jwV8bd2"}],"key":"Na0QZQxoBg"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":228,"column":1},"end":{"line":228,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":228,"column":1},"end":{"line":228,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modeling","position":{"start":{"line":228,"column":1},"end":{"line":228,"column":1}},"key":"uDtSEa5Ijz"}],"key":"b9I6gtCn2M"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":228,"column":1},"end":{"line":228,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What evolves, what can be observed, and where does an action enter?","position":{"start":{"line":228,"column":1},"end":{"line":228,"column":1}},"key":"KoyBY8XLP0"}],"key":"U9rdIjf9kj"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":228,"column":1},"end":{"line":228,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A state, action, disturbance, objective, constraint set, and model interface","position":{"start":{"line":228,"column":1},"end":{"line":228,"column":1}},"key":"nzvdzkMD85"}],"key":"vR4bpXvfdI"}],"key":"QNJRyvLVjc"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":229,"column":1},"end":{"line":229,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":229,"column":1},"end":{"line":229,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trajectory optimization","position":{"start":{"line":229,"column":1},"end":{"line":229,"column":1}},"key":"ZjpUS5EzUl"}],"key":"oXbqplTJED"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":229,"column":1},"end":{"line":229,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What action sequence performs well from one initial condition?","position":{"start":{"line":229,"column":1},"end":{"line":229,"column":1}},"key":"j6y2TaWleA"}],"key":"IpKBvPcePD"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":229,"column":1},"end":{"line":229,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"An open-loop plan","position":{"start":{"line":229,"column":1},"end":{"line":229,"column":1}},"key":"eHo7QOSV8K"}],"key":"N6FSu9XfVE"}],"key":"JkcdGBSpl2"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":230,"column":1},"end":{"line":230,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":230,"column":1},"end":{"line":230,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model predictive control","position":{"start":{"line":230,"column":1},"end":{"line":230,"column":1}},"key":"gI6zYj9tPt"}],"key":"jKjuzDPnHt"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":230,"column":1},"end":{"line":230,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"How can the plan react to new measurements?","position":{"start":{"line":230,"column":1},"end":{"line":230,"column":1}},"key":"G5OW2XmzOL"}],"key":"mxFf5wy8RA"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":230,"column":1},"end":{"line":230,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feedback through repeated replanning","position":{"start":{"line":230,"column":1},"end":{"line":230,"column":1}},"key":"vWFJz0PNvh"}],"key":"pR9WVtmf4X"}],"key":"brFZNBBIXY"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":231,"column":1},"end":{"line":231,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":231,"column":1},"end":{"line":231,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic programming","position":{"start":{"line":231,"column":1},"end":{"line":231,"column":1}},"key":"lomupgontX"}],"key":"qukHNbgcsx"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":231,"column":1},"end":{"line":231,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"How can decisions be optimized for many possible future states?","position":{"start":{"line":231,"column":1},"end":{"line":231,"column":1}},"key":"oPjkkoHtj2"}],"key":"Gmfmy4D4Qd"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":231,"column":1},"end":{"line":231,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A value function and state-contingent policy","position":{"start":{"line":231,"column":1},"end":{"line":231,"column":1}},"key":"wRIHne63Hn"}],"key":"RgLoZmaG5u"}],"key":"jZOWoCoVan"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":232,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":232,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Approximation and learning","position":{"start":{"line":232,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"key":"uOaenHOa7A"}],"key":"XZQhFjIdzr"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":232,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which required object is unavailable or too expensive to compute exactly?","position":{"start":{"line":232,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"key":"Lj3a9VNh6Q"}],"key":"N23JIxJOaG"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":232,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"An approximation learned from models, samples, or interaction","position":{"start":{"line":232,"column":1},"end":{"line":232,"column":1}},"key":"D7BM5P0aQP"}],"key":"PSPNgdjdcf"}],"key":"LsgJYfgIH5"}],"key":"qhxvzSiGyI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":234,"column":1},"end":{"line":239,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This order does not assume that a complete mechanistic model is always\navailable. It makes the information restriction explicit. Known conservation\nlaws, geometry, and constraints remain in the formulation; uncertain\nparameters, residual dynamics, value functions, or policies become learning\ntargets. Flexible function approximators are used when an uncertain or\ncomputationally intractable component must be estimated from data.","position":{"start":{"line":234,"column":1},"end":{"line":234,"column":1}},"key":"Nvw180wtfw"}],"key":"oJSaQ1iGeP"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":241,"column":1},"end":{"line":246,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dynamic programming, function approximation, and optimization provide a shared\nmathematical foundation for these methods. The same structures recur in\nreinforcement learning, control theory, operations research, and other areas of\nsequential decision-making. Their common form makes it possible to transfer an\nargument or algorithm from one setting to another while preserving the\nassumptions that make it valid.","position":{"start":{"line":241,"column":1},"end":{"line":241,"column":1}},"key":"oC6n7Dh6r1"}],"key":"QYJfYX4VHU"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":248,"column":1},"end":{"line":248,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Self-checks","position":{"start":{"line":248,"column":1},"end":{"line":248,"column":1}},"key":"wTxup3gLer"}],"identifier":"self-checks","label":"Self-checks","html_id":"self-checks","implicit":true,"key":"nuugHnxv7F"},{"type":"exercise","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Problem before algorithm","position":{"start":{"line":250,"column":1},"end":{"line":250,"column":1}},"key":"iEx3FGYMFL"}],"key":"mDXQVyg9xh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":253,"column":1},"end":{"line":253,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A production controller must respect a hard storage limit. Is selecting a learning algorithm enough to define the reinforcement-learning problem? Name two pieces of structure that must be specified first.","position":{"start":{"line":253,"column":1},"end":{"line":253,"column":1}},"key":"xkAhVgnWOm"}],"key":"r041OMYvjH"}],"label":"ex-intro-check-1","identifier":"ex-intro-check-1","enumerated":true,"enumerator":"1","html_id":"ex-intro-check-1","key":"voUDIk8Eng"},{"type":"solution","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solution to ","key":"iEvTFYdQm7"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"ex-intro-check-1","identifier":"ex-intro-check-1","kind":"exercise","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exercise ","key":"iHDyKtlzWx"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"zHLYHiSleJ"}],"template":"Exercise %s","enumerator":"1","resolved":true,"html_id":"ex-intro-check-1","key":"AidEk5tDkS"}],"key":"DTJiN4kv1k"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":259,"column":1},"end":{"line":259,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. At minimum, the state and available decisions must be defined, together with the objective and the hard storage constraint. The transition or feedback structure must also say how decisions affect future states.","position":{"start":{"line":259,"column":1},"end":{"line":259,"column":1}},"key":"ryAAfxEPAB"}],"key":"YeVeSxae2v"}],"class":"dropdown","key":"VJQV7GNPfY"},{"type":"exercise","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared structure","position":{"start":{"line":262,"column":1},"end":{"line":262,"column":1}},"key":"d8WKMdCVaw"}],"key":"Io0Gm5uBTg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":265,"column":1},"end":{"line":265,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What feature makes trajectory optimization, model predictive control, and reinforcement learning members of the same decision-making family?","position":{"start":{"line":265,"column":1},"end":{"line":265,"column":1}},"key":"fLuZvoqmGG"}],"key":"JpTp4crp1F"}],"label":"ex-intro-check-2","identifier":"ex-intro-check-2","enumerated":true,"enumerator":"2","html_id":"ex-intro-check-2","key":"mwpS01bCk8"},{"type":"solution","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solution to ","key":"KlIKswKqJG"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"ex-intro-check-2","identifier":"ex-intro-check-2","kind":"exercise","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exercise ","key":"iQMVPSJPnJ"},{"type":"text","value":"2","key":"zlUDRTZmc4"}],"template":"Exercise %s","enumerator":"2","resolved":true,"html_id":"ex-intro-check-2","key":"HJHuC8xxVf"}],"key":"YEBOjHf13Z"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":271,"column":1},"end":{"line":271,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"All three choose actions whose consequences unfold through a dynamical system over time, so present decisions must account for future objectives and constraints.","position":{"start":{"line":271,"column":1},"end":{"line":271,"column":1}},"key":"RuCzvWkW9Q"}],"key":"ZEwSDpXlBB"}],"class":"dropdown","key":"RUPJOM5TVC"},{"type":"exercise","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Course scope","position":{"start":{"line":274,"column":1},"end":{"line":274,"column":1}},"key":"C5hfo8A6qi"}],"key":"cIHLP4uC31"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":277,"column":1},"end":{"line":280,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suppose an environment already supplies a Markov state, an action set, a reward,\nand a transition sampler. Which formulation work has already been done? What\nadditional computations become possible if differentiable dynamics and hard\nconstraints are also available?","position":{"start":{"line":277,"column":1},"end":{"line":277,"column":1}},"key":"yM69gRp1uC"}],"key":"lDgK509tjK"}],"label":"ex-intro-check-3","identifier":"ex-intro-check-3","enumerated":true,"enumerator":"3","html_id":"ex-intro-check-3","key":"C3ksgsZBTI"},{"type":"solution","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solution to ","key":"S4YX6ZuBOc"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"ex-intro-check-3","identifier":"ex-intro-check-3","kind":"exercise","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exercise ","key":"U7BBl4KeXz"},{"type":"text","value":"3","key":"PjHjG8Eww4"}],"template":"Exercise %s","enumerator":"3","resolved":true,"html_id":"ex-intro-check-3","key":"oVLWii2dJj"}],"key":"PMbXrnEM7K"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":286,"column":1},"end":{"line":292,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The environment designer has selected a boundary, state representation, actions,\ntransition interface, and reward, although those choices may still need to be\naudited against the intended application. Sample-based reinforcement-learning\nmethods can operate on that interface. Differentiable dynamics and explicit\nconstraints also permit direct trajectory optimization, model predictive\ncontrol, local feedback design, and model-based dynamic programming when their\nother assumptions are satisfied.","position":{"start":{"line":286,"column":1},"end":{"line":286,"column":1}},"key":"ScQARGGIHp"}],"key":"JQlMkjkCaI"}],"class":"dropdown","key":"M3IbOVJw8i"}],"key":"KObREop87S"}],"key":"KkcKkoOK48"},"references":{"cite":{"order":["SuttonBarto2018","DulacArnold2021","Degrave2022","Brunke2022"],"data":{"SuttonBarto2018":{"label":"SuttonBarto2018","enumerator":"1","html":"Sutton, R. S., & Barto, A. G. (2018). <i>Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction</i> (2nd ed.). MIT Press. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" href=\"http://incompleteideas.net/book/the-book-2nd.html\">http://incompleteideas.net/book/the-book-2nd.html</a>","url":"http://incompleteideas.net/book/the-book-2nd.html"},"DulacArnold2021":{"label":"DulacArnold2021","enumerator":"2","doi":"10.1007/s10994-021-05961-4","html":"Dulac-Arnold, G., Levine, N., Mankowitz, D. J., Li, J., Paduraru, C., Gowal, S., & Hester, T. (2021). Challenges of Real-World Reinforcement Learning: Definitions, Benchmarks and Analysis. <i>Machine Learning</i>, <i>110</i>(9), 2419–2468. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-021-05961-4\">10.1007/s10994-021-05961-4</a>","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-021-05961-4"},"Degrave2022":{"label":"Degrave2022","enumerator":"3","doi":"10.1038/s41586-021-04301-9","html":"Degrave, J., Felici, F., Buchli, J., Neunert, M., Tracey, B., Carpanese, F., Ewalds, T., Hafner, R., Abdolmaleki, A., de las Casas, D., Donner, C., Fritz, L., Galperti, C., Huber, A., Keeling, J., Tsimpoukelli, M., Kay, J., Merle, A., Moret, J.-M., … Riedmiller, M. (2022). Magnetic Control of Tokamak Plasmas through Deep Reinforcement Learning. <i>Nature</i>, <i>602</i>(7897), 414–419. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04301-9\">10.1038/s41586-021-04301-9</a>","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04301-9"},"Brunke2022":{"label":"Brunke2022","enumerator":"4","doi":"10.1146/annurev-control-042920-020211","html":"Brunke, L., Greeff, M., Hall, A. W., Yuan, Z., Zhou, S., Panerati, J., & Schoellig, A. P. (2022). Safe Learning in Robotics: From Learning-Based Control to Safe Reinforcement Learning. <i>Annual Review of Control, Robotics, and Autonomous Systems</i>, <i>5</i>(1), 411–444. <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\" href=\"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-control-042920-020211\">10.1146/annurev-control-042920-020211</a>","url":"https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-control-042920-020211"}}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"next":{"title":"Syllabus · Plan de cours","url":"/syllabus","group":"Building Up RL: From Dynamics and Control to Learning"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}